x86_64-linux  Every difference is a definition clang had no use for, and none is the primary base's: the names the layout produces - `_ZTV1X`, `_ZN1XC1Ev`, and the C2 constructors of A, V and D1 that it calls - agree with clang, and the vtable's words are compared in the header of the .cpp. cxx1 emits `_ZN1AC1Ev`, `_ZN1VC1Ev`, `_ZN2D1C1Ev` and `_ZN1XC2Ev`, the complete-object constructors of the three bases and the base-subobject constructor of X, none of which this file calls - nothing builds an A, a V or a D1 on its own, and nothing derives from X - and with them `_ZTV2D1` and `_ZTT2D1`, D1's own group, which clang never wants because it builds D1 only ever as X's base, through the construction vtable `_ZTC1X0_2D1` both compilers emit. An unused definition is not a wrong one; cxx1 emits C1 beside every C2, the way virtual-base-diamond.nonames records for the destructors.
arm64-darwin  The same, with Mach-O's leading underscore.
